 {"id":"PERF-013","title":"P3 — the selftest gate spends ~85% of its CPU inside full 00->40 node-provision runs that assert one layer: 2.49 s CPU at d5f7fb2, layer-40 work asserted is 0.01 s","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Perf tick code-factory-perf-2026-11-03-14-51-11. Measured at d5f7fb2 (board 56 rows) with staged measure.sh + attrib.sh in ~/.hermes/cache/scratch/cf-perf-20261103-1451, /usr/bin/time -p, fresh destinations: `bash tests/selftest.sh` = 2.79 / 2.45 / 2.44 s real with user+sys 2.58 / 2.49 / 2.49 s (34 PASS rows, loadavg 4.8-5.0). The prior perf record at b365792 (07:44) was 1.38-1.54 s wall / 1.38-1.44 s CPU with 28 PASS rows, so the gate's CPU nearly doubled when sections 14 (bb99a5c) and 15 (d088bc6) landed — each runs the full 00->40 provision fixture three times. Per-arm attribution at the tip: full `--apply --repo file://` run (layers 00-40) 0.23-0.26 s CPU; `--check --project demo` (layers 00-30) 0.18 s CPU; the layer-40 work those fixtures actually assert (`git clone --depth 1 file://`) 0.01 s CPU; `python3 -c 'import venv'` 0.02 s; `python3 scripts/disk_floor.py --explain` 0.02 s. tests/selftest.sh invokes project-node.sh 13 times (~8 real runs); at ~0.21 s CPU per run that is ~2.1 s of the 2.49 s gate, ~85%. The gate is dominated by paying every layer for fixtures that assert one row.","acceptance_criteria":"1. scripts/project-node.sh accepts `--layers 20,40` (comma list of the check layers: 00,10,20,30,40), runs only the named layers, prints one `SKIP  layer NN` row per skipped check layer and exactly one `SCOPE: layers <list> (fixture mode — not a node certification)` line; an unknown layer or an empty list exits 2 with the usage line and runs no layer.\n2. A scoped run can never certify a node: under `--apply --layers ...` no .node/node.json is written even when every in-scope layer is green (assert the file is absent), and the summary line keeps its existing shape (`NODE: PASS` / `NODE: FAIL (n)`).\n3. Unscoped behaviour is byte-identical: `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` with 34 `^  PASS` rows, and `HOME=<fresh> ./scripts/project-node.sh --apply --project perfprobe --repo https://github.com/github/gitignore --ref main` still prints 3 `^  FAIL` rows and `NODE: FAIL (3)`.\n4. tests/selftest.sh sections 4, 4b, 14 and 15 pass `--layers` for the layers they assert, and each keeps its exact-row assertion (same `grep -Fxc` counts) plus a both-directions proof: the scoped fixture still FAILs when the asserted layer fails and still PASSes when it is green.\n5. Measured by the row owner with /usr/bin/time -p, three consecutive runs at the fixed tip: selftest user+sys below 1.2 s each (from 2.49 s at d5f7fb2), all rc=0, `SELFTEST: PASS`; the before/after numbers, the exact commands and the per-arm attribution go in docs/audits/code-factory-perf-2026-11-03-PERF-013.md.\n6. The commit message names AGENTS.md rule 2 (every check must be able to fail) and carries the `# ch:trace row=PERF-013` marker.","probe":"cd /home/kara/code-factory && for i in 1 2 3; do /usr/bin/time -p bash tests/selftest.sh; done 2>&1 | awk '/^real|^user|^sys/{print}'; expected: user+sys well under 1.2 s per run with SELFTEST: PASS","created_at":"2026-11-03T19:55:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T19:55:00Z","detail":{"evidence":[{"run_id":"code-factory-perf-2026-11-03-14-51-11","ts":"2026-11-03T19:55:00Z","tree_fp":"ab46ef682df4b73e51d5e82759d5bb097263cb57","artifacts":[]}]}}
 {"id":"CF-074","title":"20 of 22 complete rows have no closure-evidence entry a stranger can resolve — the doctrine has no gate, so each closure mints a fresh unresolvable digest","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 by the review lane (tick code-factory-review-2026-11-03-14-58-22) in a throwaway clone of origin/main at 64c0c10828525e091a3a8747a38fd8d2e299a81e, board read at 58 rows / 22 complete.\n\nThe repository has a written closure doctrine (docs/traceability-doctrine.md, rules 2/4/6: evidence is a produced artifact, a closing verdict binds to a PUBLISHED commit, and every exact repository-relative artifact must resolve in the published tree; plus the board's own CF-047 reading that a 40/64-hex value which `git cat-file -t` does not resolve is not evidence). Nothing enforces it. tests/selftest.sh section 7 parses the JSONL and stops there; `grep -rln 'ch:trace' scripts tests` matches only selftest.sh, and no script validates detail.evidence. The result is a per-closure drift that today covers almost every closed row:\n\nA mechanical audit of every COMPLETE row's detail.evidence (script and full transcript reproduced in the lane evidence file, /home/kara/.hermes/stand-in/review/code-factory/review-2026-11-03-evidence.md) finds only TWO complete rows with an evidence entry a stranger can resolve in a fresh clone: CF-034 and CF-055. The other 20 of 22 are unresolvable, and not for exotic reasons:\n\n1. One single fingerprint, 59f476975880e46d49eec1fbaec75b289ced048f4e669855d2cee386061fb5ec, is pasted across EIGHT closures (CF-009, CF-010, CF-014, CF-015, CF-030, CF-032, CF-033, CF-035). `git cat-file -t` on it prints \"fatal: Not a valid object name\"; no document anywhere in the tree states the algorithm that would regenerate it (`grep -rn tree_fp --include='*.md'` returns no definition). Eight closures therefore rest on one value that resolves to nothing and cannot be recomputed.\n2. Five more complete rows carry a 64-hex tree_fp that names no git object and is named by no pending row: CF-012 (690b81d713dc…), CF-033 second entry, CF-039 (8b3e799f6ddc…), and CF-032's first entry. (CF-036/CF-037/CF-038/CF-041/CF-052/CF-054/CF-058/PERF-012 are the same class but are already named one-by-one by CF-045/046/047/059/067/073.)\n3. CF-032's second evidence entry lists NO artifacts at all (artifacts: []) and its run_id 0576a54ecb90ce3155df1fdc984a38cf7836e92a resolves only in the owner's canonical checkout through the local ref refs/gitreins/history — a fresh clone of the published repo prints \"fatal: Not a valid object name\". Doctrine rule 4 disqualifies exactly that: a local unpushed ref does not close a finding. (This also falsifies one sentence in CF-046's reasoning, which cites 0576a54e as the published exemplar; evidence appended to CF-046.)\n4. CF-011/CF-013 list a pruned absolute host path (CF-045's class) together with an unresolvable fingerprint.\n\nThe pattern matters more than any single row: each of the individual rows named above was closed by a lane that ran a real test (the substance is often sound — this tick independently confirmed the CF-054 fix and its verdict), and then recorded a fingerprint that no reviewer can resolve. Filing one row per instance produces 20 near-duplicate rows and leaves the generator running. This row is the generator: the missing invariant and its gate. Wiring the audit into tests/selftest.sh makes a re-run prove both the current backlog and any future closure.\n\nIndependent checks run this tick at b430bed: `bash tests/selftest.sh` -> SELFTEST: PASS (rc=0, 2.50 s); gitreins guard Tier 1 DEGRADED PASS (board-only change, secrets clean). The audit script used here exits 1 today and is reproduced verbatim in the lane evidence file so the implementer can adopt it rather than re-derive it.","acceptance_criteria":"1. A committed, re-runnable audit script (e.g. scripts/closure_audit.py) takes the board path and a published ref and exits 1 while any COMPLETE row has no evidence entry whose artifacts resolve in `git ls-tree -r <ref>` AND whose hex tree_fp resolves via `git cat-file -t` to a git object and is not all zeros; it exits 0 when every complete row has one such entry. Re-run today at origin/main it must exit 1 naming at least the 20 row ids in this row's reasoning (CF-009,010,011,012,013,014,015,030,032,033,035,036,037,038,039,041,052,054,058,PERF-012); after the amendments it must exit 0. Paste both invocations and both exit codes into the evidence artifact.\n\n2. The audit is wired into tests/selftest.sh as its own numbered section and the wiring is proven causal: with the pre-fix board (origin/main at b430bed, or a copy of it) the section prints FAIL naming >=20 row ids and the run exits nonzero; with the amended board it prints PASS. Record both runs, including the section's own output lines, in the evidence artifact.\n\n3. The shared fingerprint 59f476975880e46d49eec1fbaec75b289ced048f4e669855d2cee386061fb5ec is retired the documented way for all eight rows that carry it (CF-009, CF-010, CF-014, CF-015, CF-030, CF-032, CF-033, CF-035): each row either gains an appended evidence entry whose fingerprint `git cat-file -t <fp>` resolves to a tree, or records `none:<reason>` PLUS the exact command (algorithm) that regenerates the value at HEAD and the value it regenerates to. A bare re-paste of the same unresolvable digest does not satisfy this criterion.\n\n4. CF-032's second evidence entry stops resting on an unpublished ref: its run_id either names a commit that `git cat-file -t` resolves in a FRESH clone of origin (not only via refs/gitreins/history in the owner's checkout) or is superseded by an appended entry with `none:<reason>`, and its empty artifacts[] is replaced by at least one repository-relative artifact that `git cat-file -e <ref>:<path>` resolves.\n\n5. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS at the closing commit, and `jq -c . .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` parses every line with no duplicate ids; the closing commit is pushed and the row's commit_hash names it.","created_at":"2026-11-03T15:02:06Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T15:02:06Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"223a2eab36ad3380a974a18b4e9a93341fc680782194f5fb5fdf9fcf27b83269"}}
